.TH TOUCH 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
touch \- change file access and modification times
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B touch
.RB [ \-achm ]
.RB [ \-d
.I date
|
.B \-r
.I ref
|
.B \-t
.IR time ]
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B touch
sets the access and modification times of each
.IR file ,
by default to the current time, using
.BR utimensat (2).
.PP
A
.I file
that does not exist is created as an empty regular file with mode 0666
(modified by the umask), unless
.B \-c
or
.B \-h
is given.
A directory operand is not an error; only its times are changed.
.PP
When more than one of
.BR \-r ,
.B \-d
and
.B \-t
is given,
.B \-r
takes precedence over
.BR \-d ,
which takes precedence over
.BR \-t .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Change only the access time (unless
.B \-m
is also given).
.TP
.B \-m
Change only the modification time (unless
.B \-a
is also given).
.TP
.B \-c
Do not create files that do not exist.
.TP
.B \-h
Change the times of a symbolic link itself rather than its target
.RB ( AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW ).
With
.BR \-r ,
the reference times are also taken from the link itself.
Implies that missing files are not created.
.TP
.BI \-d " date"
Use
.I date
instead of the current time.
Accepted forms are
.BI @ seconds
(seconds since the Epoch),
.IR YYYY \- MM \- DD
(midnight), and
.IR YYYY \- MM \- DD\fBT\fIhh : mm : ss .
Dates are interpreted in local time.
.TP
.BI \-r " ref"
Use the access and modification times of file
.IR ref ,
including nanoseconds.
.TP
.BI \-t " time"
Use
.I time
in the POSIX form
.RI [[ CC ] YY ] MMDDhhmm [. SS ],
interpreted in local time.
With no year the current year is used; a two-digit year
.I YY
below 69 means 20\fIYY\fR, otherwise 19\fIYY\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All files were updated.
.TP
.B 1
An option or date was invalid, no
.I file
was given,
.I ref
could not be read, or at least one
.I file
could not be created or updated.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create an empty file or update its times to now:
.EX
$ touch stamp
.EE
.PP
Copy the times of another file:
.EX
$ touch \-r configure config.h.in
.EE
.PP
Set a specific time:
.EX
$ touch \-t 202601011200.00 file
$ touch \-d @0 file
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ls (1),
.BR stat (2),
.BR utimensat (2)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024, with
.B \-h
and the
.B @
form of
.B \-d
as extensions.
.SH BUGS
A
.B \-d
date of the form
.I "YYYY-MM-DD hh:mm:ss"
(with a space) is accepted, but the time of day is silently ignored and
midnight is used.
Trailing garbage after a recognised
.B \-d
date is ignored.
.PP
The usage message does not mention
.BR \-h .
